Id: inet005f
Severity: FAIL
Category: services
Check: check_inetd
Fix: Disable the unit, xinetd service or inetd.conf line, and remove the package; SSH does the same job encrypted.
References: in.telnetd(8); rshd(8); in.tftpd(8); xinetd.conf(5); inetd.conf(5); systemd.socket(5); CIS Distribution Independent Linux Benchmark 2.2 (Special Purpose Services)
Controls: CIS 4.8, 3.10; NIST CM-7, SC-8; ISO 8.21, 5.14; CE Secure configuration

A telnet, rsh/rlogin/rexec or TFTP server is enabled on this Linux
host: its systemd service or socket is enabled, xinetd offers it
(a service in /etc/xinetd.d not set to disable = yes, with xinetd
enabled or running), an inetd.conf line names it with inetd enabled or
running, or the program itself is running. telnet carries logins and
everything typed after them across the network in clear; rsh, rlogin
and rexec take the client's word for who is calling (hosts.equiv,
.rhosts) and are cleartext too; TFTP moves files with no authentication
at all. The CIS Benchmarks ask for all of them to be off or absent, and
no distribution installs one by default.

The detail names what enables it. Turn it off ('systemctl disable
--now telnet.socket'; 'disable = yes' in the xinetd.d file; the line
commented out of /etc/inetd.conf, then the super-server restarted) and
remove the package: SSH and scp do the same work encrypted, and a TFTP
server belongs on a PXE server behind a firewall and nowhere else.
